//// A local `val` declared in one top-level body is still visible to the
//// CHECKER in the body of every top-level declaration that appears after it.
////
//// `holder`'s `v` is a local. `later` is a different function and names `v`
//// with nothing in scope to bind it to — and it TYPE-CHECKS. The program then
//// dies at run time on commonJS and at `erlc` on erlang.
////
//// Order is the whole defect: move `later` ABOVE `holder` and the same module
//// is correctly rejected.

fn holder() -> string {
    val v = "inner";
    return v;
}

fn later() -> string {
    return v;
}

test "a name bound in another function's body compiles here" {
    assert later() == "inner";
}
